  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Express $\\frac{3x+5}{(x-3)(2x+1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n|  | $\\frac{3x+5}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\\,=\\,\\frac{2}{x-3}\\,\\,+$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $2x+1$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+5}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{2}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n3x+5=2(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-\\frac{1}{2})+5 & =2(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+5}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{2}{x-3}+\\frac{-1}{2x+1}.\n$$",
